WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.103
Film Courage: Was there a blog post [scriptcat.wordpress]
or chapter in the book [Mark’s new book

00:00:03.103 --> 00:00:04.103
A Screenwriter's Journey to Success: Tips,
tricks and tactics to survive as a working

00:00:04.103 --> 00:00:05.103
screenwriter in Hollywood] that was actually
difficult for you to write?

00:00:05.103 --> 00:00:09.320
So I know your book is a culmination of these
blog posts that you’ve collected over a

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:10.320
5-year period.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:13.099
But was there one where you thought “Oh,
this is fine.”

00:00:13.099 --> 00:00:16.949
And then you realized “You know, [writing
it] this is actually affecting me.”

00:00:16.949 --> 00:00:19.230
Mark Sanderson: Ah…interesting.

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:29.590
Yeah, I think going back to the slow periods
in one’s career or having achieved something

00:00:29.590 --> 00:00:35.590
and not having the results that you think…like
“Look, I sold a movie.

00:00:35.590 --> 00:00:37.070
I should be this.”

00:00:37.070 --> 00:00:41.440
And the reality is well no, that’s not always
the case.

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:48.980
After I sold my first spec, it was my fifth
spec but it was my first spec that I sold,

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:53.620
I was at the time doing catering because I
worked in restaurants before I went to film

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:58.730
school and after [film school] because I didn’t
want to get into a day job where I would not

00:00:58.730 --> 00:01:03.790
have any time to write and so a restaurant
was good money and it would free you up to

00:01:03.790 --> 00:01:06.600
be able to write (as long as you would write).

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:07.600
Because that’s important.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:12.000
I was in the restaurant with a lot of people
wanting to be actors and they loved playing

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:14.800
the role of an actors, but not actually doing
the work.

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:22.580
So if you’re given the free time to do it,
you better do it and I always did it.

00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:29.560
But it was the time when I sold my spec and
I was in the trade papers (Variety) and every

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:30.970
writer wants to be in Variety, right?

00:01:30.970 --> 00:01:33.520
Then you say “I made it,” right?

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:39.040
Well the day that I was in Variety, the headline
was this new company had formed and they optioned

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:42.600
my script and they were going to buy it and
make it into their first movie.

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:49.140
And that day in Variety I had to do a catering
job at one of the biggest producer’s in

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:51.280
Hollywood’s house.

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:55.600
And so it was this catering company and my
friend and I drove up to Beverly Hills and

00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:01.780
here we are at this giant mansion of this
producer and it was a big breakfast meeting

00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:12.650
with big power players, and I was thinking
“Wow?

00:02:12.650 --> 00:02:14.340
I’m in Variety today?

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:16.970
Why should I be catering?”

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:22.000
And also I was thinking in a way (which story
is in the book) “What if this producer had

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:23.950
rejected my screenplay?”

00:02:23.950 --> 00:02:26.620
Here I am in his house, serving him coffee,
you know?

00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:33.950
So just be careful of who you disrespect on
the way up or down because you just might

00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:36.640
end up in their house pouring coffee for them.

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:41.650
That was hard to write [in the book] and I
look back on that now and I laugh.

00:02:41.650 --> 00:02:48.820
But it’s the reality of the business because
as you know, it’s a step deal.

00:02:48.820 --> 00:02:54.270
So when anything sells, it’s in steps and
you may not get the final steps.

00:02:54.270 --> 00:02:58.099
When a screenplay sells you get a little bit
of money and then a little bit of money from

00:02:58.099 --> 00:02:59.099
the next draft and the next.

00:02:59.099 --> 00:03:02.319
And it has to be made for you to get the bonus.

00:03:02.319 --> 00:03:07.290
So everyone thinks that just because a script
sells, suddenly everybody’s calling on the

00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:09.380
phone and things like that.

00:03:09.380 --> 00:03:16.540
I would say that was not difficult to revisit
but I think about the time and I put myself

00:03:16.540 --> 00:03:17.540
back into the time.

00:03:17.540 --> 00:03:22.099
That’s what I’m trying to do when I was
writing because I’m a very visual person

00:03:22.099 --> 00:03:30.060
and I can really have the sense memory and
back in the time it wasn’t funny, you know?

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:35.730
It was like “Oh my gosh, so now I have done
this and still…I have to do this?”

00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:40.040
And you’re like yeah, many times you have
to live in these two parallel worlds and that

00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:41.129
can mess with your head a lot.

00:03:41.129 --> 00:03:48.710
So you have to be very grounded and say this
is the reality of it and it’s not the image

00:03:48.710 --> 00:03:50.330
of who I am.

00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:55.540
I am a writer, if I’m catering or always
doing something else at the time.

00:03:55.540 --> 00:04:00.480
Film Courage: You know, I think this is such
an important story because I think people

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:03.900
can be caught in two separate worlds here
[in Los Angeles] for years.

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:04.900
Mark Sanderson: Absolutely.

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:09.959
Film Courage: And eventually some will definitely
turn the corner and the party will be at their

00:04:09.959 --> 00:04:14.380
house (so to speak, to use this metaphor)
and unfortunately for some, it won’t.

